          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why is it hard to keep taking a preventer inhaler every day when you feel fine?",
          "answer": "The information available does not directly explain the psychological and physiological reasons why it can be difficult for individuals to consistently use a preventer inhaler when they are not experiencing symptoms.\n\nHowever, the clinical reference corpus highlights that issues with inhaler technique and adherence are common. For instance, the document \"Sticking with asthma treatment: inhaler technique, controllers and action plans\" (lifestyle-asthma-adherence) states that poor technique can make even faithful daily use ineffective. Also, the \"Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy\" guideline (guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy) mentions that technique and adherence are checked before increasing medication, as they can be reasons for treatment appearing to fail. This suggests that adherence, even in general, is a known challenge in managing conditions requiring inhalers.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
